  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled conditions today into Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAt the surface, troffing will persist across the region today \ninto tonight. Aloft, a disturbance in advance of low pressure \nacross the Great Lakes will cross the region today. With \nsupport from the surface trof, upper level disturbance and \ndiurnal heating scattered showers along with isolated/scattered \nthunderstorms will develop again today from late morning into \nthe afternoon. Patchy fog is also possible this morning. The \nGreat Lakes system will approach tonight keeping a chance of \nshowers across the forecast area. The surface/upper system then \nbegins to cross the region Friday with rain. Could also have \nthe slight chance of an afternoon thunderstorm.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather and a gradual warming trend \nis expected into early next week, with frequent chances of \nafternoon showers and thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nPositive PNA teleconnection pattern keeps ridging over the west,\nand slowly funnels low pressure systems into the northeast,\nresulting in the persistent rain showers. This weekend, low\npressure system departs to the northeast, and surface high\npressure system continues to reside west. Shortwaves swing into\nMaine Saturday and Sunday, supporting the potential for some \nrain showers and a few thunderstorms. Kept thunder at slight \nchance this weekend, as not anticipating much in terms of \nclearing until early next week. There is some CAPE and moisture,\nbut lapse rates are modest and there is not much forcing. With \ngradually warming trend this week, temperatures begin to raise \ninto the low-to-mid 80s, and diurnal heating could provide lift \nfor convection to kick off. There is a chance for some isolated \nsevere storms, but primary threats seem to be sub-severe \nthunderstorms with gusty winds, small hail, some bursts of rain,\nand lightning.",
